LESSONS_P6 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Purchase Receipt: scope and position", "11", "<p>This module covers the Purchase Receipt and the receiving function in ZhiftERP. On completing this module you will be able to: complete the Purchase Receipt field walk; run the dock discipline — counting, damage identification, and identity capture at the truck; operate the accepted and rejected lanes with the supplier claim procedure; state what submission posts to stock and accounting; run quality holds and release for the classes that require them; and run the receiving reads.</p><p><b>What a Purchase Receipt is.</b> The Purchase Receipt records a physical arrival of goods: which items, in what quantities, from which supplier's despatch, into which warehouse, on which date — with what came damaged, what identity numbers arrived, and who counted. It is the inbound custody-transfer document: before submission the goods are the supplier's despatch; after submission they are the business's stock at the receipt's figures. It is also the middle document of the three-way match — ordered, received, billed — and the matching control (module 7) is only as good as the receipt's honesty.</p><p><b>Position in the flow.</b> The receipt sits between the Purchase Order and the Purchase Invoice:</p><p>1. <b>Created against the order</b> — the receipt draws the order's rows and receives against them: %received advances per row, incoming falls, and the trace runs order → receipt.<br>2. <b>Feeding the match</b> — the invoice (module 7) compares to the order's rates and the receipt's quantities; the receipt states what the business actually holds and therefore what claims it should accept.<br>3. <b>Feeding stock</b> — submission posts the goods into the warehouse at the receipt's figures, with the identity the recall trace and expiry management run on (the Inventory track consumes what this module captures).</p><p><b>The one-sentence standard.</b> The standing rule the whole module serves: <b>the receipt states what physically arrived — counted at the dock, split by condition, identified by lot, dated when it happened — never what the paperwork says should have arrived.</b> The supplier's delivery note and the business's order are the references the count is compared against; the receipt records the count.</p><p><b>Custody.</b> The receiving function counts and posts; the dock procedure requires the driver's presence through the count for disputed-at-truck findings; quality holds run per the class rules with the compliance owner's release; claims against suppliers file with their evidence; and receipts post on the day goods arrive, per the honest-date rule.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the field walk. Chapter 3: the dock discipline. Chapter 4: accepted and rejected. Chapter 5: what submission posts. Chapter 6: quality and release. Chapter 7: the receiving reads. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Purchase Receipt doctype", "12", "<p>Create the Purchase Receipt from the Purchase Order (Create &gt; Purchase Receipt) so the rows and references carry. Complete or verify the fields in order.</p><p><b>1. Supplier and the order reference.</b> Carried by creation; the supplier's delivery note number enters the reference field — the document the count was compared against, and the number every claim and query cites.</p><p><b>2. Posting Date and Time.</b> When the goods physically arrived. The honest-date rule in its inbound form: the receipt posts on arrival day — a receipt posted days later carries stock the shelf held unrecorded (counts fail against it) and delays the received-not-billed accrual the accountant reads.</p><p><b>3. The items table.</b> Per row, drawn from the order:</p><p>1. <b>Received Quantity</b> — what the dock counted, in the row's unit, per the pairing rule (cartons counted against carton rows). The received figure may be less than ordered (the short receipt), and the row records what arrived.<br>2. <b>Accepted and Rejected Quantities</b> — the condition split from the dock decision (Chapter 3): the received quantity divides into accepted (into the stock warehouse) and rejected (into the rejected warehouse), and the two fields post to their two destinations.<br>3. <b>Warehouse and Rejected Warehouse</b> — the accepted destination per the row (Central in the standing pattern), and the rejected lane's warehouse (the quarantine or rejected location the site names).<br>4. <b>Rate</b> — carried from the order. The receipt does not negotiate: a supplier's price change is a matching matter (module 7) or an arrangement amendment (module 4), never a receiving-time edit — the receipt's rate is the order's rate so the match has its reference.<br>5. <b>Serial and batch capture</b> — controlled items enter with their identity: the batch numbers and expiry dates on the physical cartons, the serial numbers on serialised units, per the Inventory track's dock rules. The identity captured here is what the recall trace, the expiry horizons, and the warranty records run on for the stock's whole life.</p><p><b>4. Save, verify, submit.</b> The receipt is completed at the dock while the delivery is present, verified against the count sheets, and submitted on the day. Submission posts the stock and accounting events (Chapter 5); corrections after submission follow the document rules — the same physically-moved test as every custody document: cancellation only where the posting was wholly wrong on the day, the return-to-supplier lane where goods must physically go back.</p><p><b>Verification per receipt.</b> The order and delivery-note references; the honest date; received quantities as counted; the accepted/rejected split per the dock decision; identity complete on controlled rows; rates untouched from the order.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The dock discipline", "13", "<p>The receiving procedure at the truck. Each step has an owner and a record, and the sequence is fixed because each step protects the next.</p><p><b>1. Before the truck: the expected-arrivals plan.</b> The weekly incoming read (module 5) gives the dock its plan: what is due, in what quantities, with what identity requirements. A delivery nobody expected is itself a flag — checked against the order book before unloading, because unordered goods are either a supplier error or the beginning of an invoice for goods nobody bought.</p><p><b>2. The count.</b> Goods count at the dock, physically, before the driver leaves:</p><p>1. <b>Against the order and the delivery note</b> — the two references; the count is the fact. Cartons count as cartons with sample-opening per the policy (the class's sampling rate: sealed manufacturer cartons sample-open; mixed or repacked consignments open fully).<br>2. <b>In the row's units</b> — the pairing rule at its origin point: the count sheet records quantity and unit together, and the carton-versus-unit confusion that mis-posts receipts is prevented here or nowhere.<br>3. <b>Recorded on the count sheet</b> — the physical record the receipt is entered from, filed with the receipt.</p><p><b>3. The condition inspection.</b> While the driver is present: visible damage identified carton by carton — crushed, wet, broken seals, temperature indicators where the class carries them. Damaged units are set aside and counted separately: they become the rejected quantity (Chapter 4), and the finding is recorded <b>on the driver's copy of the delivery note, signed by both parties</b> — the at-truck record that makes the supplier claim a documented fact rather than a later assertion. Photographs per the claims procedure.</p><p><b>4. Identity capture.</b> For controlled items, at the dock, from the physical cartons:</p><p>1. <b>Batch numbers and expiry dates</b> — read from the manufacturer's printing, entered per lot: a consignment carrying two manufacturing lots enters as two batch lines, each with its own expiry, because mixing lots into one line destroys the recall trace at its source.<br>2. <b>The expiry gate</b> — arriving expiry dates check against the policy's minimum-remaining-life rule: stock arriving with less than the class's required horizon is a rejection or a documented commercial decision, made now at the dock, not discovered at the shelf.<br>3. <b>Serial capture</b> — serialised units scanned or recorded individually, per the Inventory rules.</p><p><b>5. The driver's departure.</b> The driver leaves with the signed delivery note stating what was received and what was disputed — quantities confirmed, damage noted, shortages recorded. After the truck leaves, the business's evidence is whatever was signed; the discipline's fixed sequence exists because every later conversation with the supplier is conducted from this document.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Accepted and rejected", "12", "<p>The received quantity divides by condition at the dock. This chapter is the two lanes and the claim procedure.</p><p><b>1. The accepted lane.</b> Sound goods, in date per the expiry gate, matching the order: the accepted quantity posts into the stock warehouse at submission and becomes sellable inventory (or enters the quality hold first, where the class requires it — Chapter 6).</p><p><b>2. The rejected lane.</b> Damaged, out-of-specification, or expiry-gate failures: the rejected quantity posts into the <b>rejected warehouse</b> — a named location whose stock is visibly not sellable, mirroring the quarantine pattern the Inventory track defined. The rejected lane's rules:</p><p>1. <b>Rejected stock is held, not hidden</b> — it remains on the books at the rejected location until its resolution, because goods physically present must be recorded present, whatever their condition.<br>2. <b>Resolution by one of three lanes</b> — return to the supplier (the return document per the site's flow, the physical send-back recorded), replacement (the supplier's replacement receiving as its own arrival, cross-referenced), or the credit claim (the rejected goods disposed per agreement, the credit note expected against the invoice — module 7's matching accounts for it).<br>3. <b>Every rejection is a claim</b> — raised with the supplier on the day, citing the signed delivery note and the photographs, with the resolution lane agreed and its deadline tracked by the open-claims read (Chapter 7).</p><p><b>3. Short receipts.</b> The truck carries less than the delivery note states, or the note states less than the order: the receipt records what arrived (the standing rule), the shortage is noted on the signed copy, and the order row remains open for the balance — the supplier delivers the remainder, or the order amends per module 5's taxonomy with the shortfall's cause recorded. What never happens: receiving paper quantities to match documents — the receipt that records 180 because the note says 180 while the truck carried 177 corrupts stock, the match, and the claim in one act.</p><p><b>4. Over-receipts.</b> The truck carries more than ordered: the over-receipt tolerance (the control-register setting, low, with the exception role named) admits genuine packing arithmetic; beyond it, the excess is refused at the dock or accepted only by the named role's decision with the order amended — unordered goods accepted casually become unmatched invoice lines and, repeated, a supplier habit.</p><p><b>5. The claims file.</b> Per claim: the signed note, the photographs, the receipt's rejected line, the correspondence, the agreed lane, the resolution and its date. Claims are money — the rejected cartons are paid-for goods until the claim resolves — and the open-claims read works the file to zero with ages visible.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — What submission posts", "12", "<p>Submission converts the counted, split, identified receipt into stock and accounting events. This chapter states each posting.</p><p><b>1. The stock posting.</b> Per row, Stock Ledger Entries: the accepted quantity into the stock warehouse, the rejected quantity into the rejected warehouse — both at the receipt's rate, because rejected goods are still the business's cost until their claim resolves. The Bins update; for controlled items, the batch and serial balances create with the captured identity — the lot lines, expiries, and serials entering the registers the Inventory track operates from this moment on.</p><p><b>2. The projected-quantity update.</b> The received quantities leave the incoming figures: %received advances on the order rows; projected quantity converts its pipeline component into actual stock. The reorder machinery's arithmetic stays true through the transition — the arrival neither double-counts (incoming falling as actual rises) nor disappears.</p><p><b>3. The accounting posting.</b> Under perpetual inventory: the stock account debits at the receipt's value, and the offsetting credit posts to the received-not-billed account — the liability that says goods are held whose invoice has not yet posted. When module 7's invoice posts, it clears this account against the supplier's payable: the two-step structure is what lets the books state, at any moment, both what stock the business holds and what claims it has accepted — separately and truthfully. The received-not-billed balance is therefore a working figure the accountant reads at each close, and the module 5 read that chases missing invoices keeps it short.</p><p><b>4. The valuation tie.</b> The receipt's rate is the valuation's starting figure (module 2's third cost): domestic included-delivery lines are substantially complete at receipt; import lines gain their acquisition layer afterward through the Landed Cost Voucher (the Inventory mechanism), which re-rates the receipt's stock with the actual freight, duty, and clearing — the worksheet-versus-actual reconciliation from module 2 closing per consignment.</p><p><b>5. Corrections after submission.</b> The lanes, per the physically-moved test:</p><p>1. <b>The posting was wrong on the day</b> (wrong warehouse selected, a count-sheet transcription error caught same-day): cancel and re-post with the honest figures, the reason recorded.<br>2. <b>Goods must physically go back</b> — the return-to-supplier lane: its own document recording the physical send-back, never a cancellation of an arrival that happened.<br>3. <b>The identity was captured wrong</b> (a batch number transposed): the correction per the Inventory track's identity procedures, because downstream documents may already reference the lot.</p><p><b>6. The tie-out participation.</b> Receipt postings are part of the monthly stock-to-accounts agreement: a tie-out difference tracing to receiving is read through this chapter's postings — the stock entry, the received-not-billed entry, and the document, together.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Quality and release", "12", "<p>Some classes do not go straight to shelf. This chapter is the hold-and-release structure.</p><p><b>1. The quality hold pattern.</b> Per the class rules the compliance owner maintains: classes requiring inspection before sale (temperature-sensitive lines, classes with regulatory release requirements, first receipts from new suppliers, trial orders per module 4's award conditions) receive into the <b>quarantine warehouse</b> rather than free stock:</p><p>1. The accepted-quantity posting targets quarantine for these classes — the goods are the business's stock, visible and counted, but not sellable.<br>2. The inspection runs per the class's procedure — the checks, the sampling, the documentation — by the roles the procedure names.<br>3. <b>Release is a movement with a decision</b>: the transfer from quarantine to free stock, posted by the release role, with the inspection record filed. Stock does not drift out of quarantine; it is released.</p><p><b>2. The pharmaceutical specifics.</b> The standing case's class rules:</p><p>1. <b>The expiry horizons</b> — the dock gate (Chapter 3) enforced arrival life; the quarantine inspection confirms lot documentation where the class requires it (the manufacturer's certificates per the compliance file).<br>2. <b>Cold-chain classes</b> — the temperature record travels with the consignment; a broken indicator is a rejection at the dock or a quarantine-and-inspect decision per the procedure, never a shrug.<br>3. <b>The compliance file per lot</b> — the certificates, the inspection record, the release decision: the file the regulator, the recall procedure, and the customer complaint all read.</p><p><b>3. Failed inspections.</b> The quarantined goods that fail: the rejection converts (the stock moves to the rejected lane), the claim raises per Chapter 4 with the inspection record as evidence, and the resolution lanes apply. A failure pattern by supplier or class is a scorecard fact (module 8's quality dimension) and, recurring, an award-criteria fact for the next cycle.</p><p><b>4. The hold reads.</b> Weekly, with the receiving reads: quarantine contents by age against the class's release windows — stock aging in quarantine is either an inspection backlog (a resourcing question) or a forgotten release (a procedure question), and both are visible because quarantine is a warehouse the reads see, not a corner of the dock. The release records sample monthly with the receipt audit.</p><p><b>5. The boundary.</b> The compliance owner rules which classes hold and what release requires; the receiving function routes per the rules; the Inventory track's quarantine machinery (module 1's warehouse, module 3's identity) is the infrastructure. This module's contribution is the inbound routing and the discipline that the rules are applied at the dock, every arrival, without judgment calls at the truck.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The receiving reads", "12", "<p>The standing reads on the receiving function, each with its cadence and action.</p><p><b>1. The expected-arrivals plan — weekly.</b> Module 5's incoming read consumed as the dock's plan: due rows by day, quantities, identity requirements, and the unexpected-delivery rule standing (unordered arrivals checked before unloading).</p><p><b>2. The receipts-pending-invoice read — weekly.</b> Module 5's received-not-billed read operated from the receiving side: submitted receipts whose invoices have not arrived within the cadence, the supplier chased for the claim, the accrual position handed to the accountant at close. The read feeds module 7's matching queue.</p><p><b>3. The open-claims read — weekly.</b> Every unresolved rejection and shortage claim by age: the agreed lane, the deadline, the next action. Claims are worked to zero because each is paid-for goods (or short-delivered goods) awaiting money or replacement — the read's aging column is naira aging, and claims resolving by replacement track into the affected drops' schedules.</p><p><b>4. The receipt audit — monthly.</b> A sample of the month's receipts checked for the discipline's evidence: posting dates against arrival dates (the honest-date check), count sheets attached, accepted/rejected splits with their signed notes where rejections occurred, identity complete on controlled rows (lots separated, expiries entered), rates untouched from orders. Findings correct the procedure or the training per the standing pattern, and the audit files with the month's records.</p><p><b>5. The receiving-performance figures — monthly, feeding module 8.</b> Per supplier, from the receipts: on-time performance (arrival dates against confirmed dates, per drop), quantity fidelity (short and over receipts), and condition (rejection rates by cause). These figures are the scorecard's receiving-side inputs — measured at the dock, from documents, per the module 8 weightings — and the monthly compilation is where the dock's records become the relationship review's evidence.</p><p><b>6. The exception patterns — quarterly.</b> The quarter's receiving exceptions aggregated: damage concentrated by supplier, route, or packaging (routed to the claims history and the carrier conversation), short-receipt patterns (routed to the supplier review), unexpected deliveries (routed to the order discipline), and quarantine aging (routed to the resourcing or procedure question). Data with names, filed with the quarterly cycle alongside the module 8 scorecard compilation.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: drop one arrives", "13", "<p>This chapter receives the first drop of the module 5 order: <b>180 cartons of PARA-500 from Emzor</b>, due this week at Central Warehouse. Values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>Step 1 — the plan and the truck (Chapter 3).</b> The expected-arrivals plan carries the drop: 180 cartons, batch-controlled, expiry-gated. The truck arrives with Emzor's delivery note stating 180; the dock counts against the note and the order.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the count and the condition split (Chapters 3-4).</b> The physical count: <b>180 cartons on the truck — 177 sound, 3 crushed</b> at the bottom of one stack. The damage is identified while the driver is present: the three cartons set aside, photographed, and recorded on the driver's copy of the delivery note, signed by both parties — 180 received, 3 damaged noted. The count sheet records quantities in cartons throughout, per the pairing rule.</p><p><b>Step 3 — identity capture (Chapter 3).</b> The consignment carries <b>two manufacturing lots</b>, and the dock enters them as two batch lines with their printed expiry dates — one of them <b>batch B-1147</b>, the lot whose register entry the Inventory track's recall procedures will later exercise. Both lots pass the expiry gate with the class's required horizon remaining. The two-lot separation is the recall trace's foundation: one blended line here would have made the later lot-level freeze impossible to execute cleanly.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the receipt posts (Chapters 2, 5).</b> The Purchase Receipt creates from the order's first row: received 180, <b>accepted 177 into Central, rejected 3 into the rejected warehouse</b>, both at the order's ₦9,600 carton rate; the delivery-note reference and count sheet attach; the posting date is arrival day. Submission posts the stock entries (177 sellable at Central; 3 held visible at rejected), creates the two batch balances with expiries, advances %received on the order (177 of 300 net of the claim's outcome), converts the drop's incoming into actual — the auto-trigger's arithmetic staying true — and posts the received-not-billed liability the invoice will clear.</p><p><b>Step 5 — the claim (Chapter 4).</b> The claim raises with Emzor the same day: the signed note, the photographs, the receipt's rejected line. The agreed lane: <b>replacement — three cartons added to drop two</b>, which amends per module 5's schedule taxonomy from 120 to 123 with the reason recorded and Emzor's confirmation updated. The claims file tracks the resolution; the rejected three remain visible at the rejected warehouse until the return-or-dispose step the agreement states, and the open-claims read carries the item until closed.</p><p><b>Step 6 — the figures file (Chapter 7).</b> The month's receiving-performance compilation records the drop: on time against its confirmed date; 180 of 180 quantity fidelity; a 1.7%-of-drop damage entry by cause (transit crush) — the figures module 8's scorecard will weigh. The receipt audit's sample later confirms the discipline's evidence complete: honest date, count sheet, signed note, two lots separated, rates untouched.</p><p><b>The handoff.</b> Drop two — now 123 cartons — holds its confirmed date; Emzor's invoice for drop one is en route to module 7's three-way match, where 177 accepted cartons at ₦9,600, a 3-carton claim, and a 180-carton invoice will meet the matching discipline.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in receiving, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — receiving the paperwork.</b> Symptom: receipts entered from delivery notes without counting; the note's 180 posted while the truck carried 177; stock, the match, and the claim corrupted in one act. Correction: the physical position re-established by count; the documents corrected through their lanes. Prevention: the count as the fact, the note as the reference — the module's one-sentence standard.</p><p><b>Error 2 — the driver gone before the record.</b> Symptom: damage and shortages discovered after departure; claims reduced to assertions; suppliers declining undocumented findings. Correction: the claim attempted on the evidence that exists. Prevention: the fixed dock sequence — count, inspect, record, sign — completed while the driver is present.</p><p><b>Error 3 — blended lots.</b> Symptom: multi-lot consignments entered as one batch line; expiries averaged or guessed; the recall trace broken at its source. Correction: the identity corrected per the Inventory procedures while the physical separation is still known. Prevention: one batch line per manufacturing lot, entered from the printed cartons at the dock.</p><p><b>Error 4 — the rate edited at receiving.</b> Symptom: receipt rates changed to match a supplier's new price or an expected invoice; the match comparing a document to itself; the award governance bypassed at the dock. Correction: the rate restored; the price question routed to matching or the arrangement. Prevention: the receipt carries the order's rate, without exception.</p><p><b>Error 5 — the invisible rejection.</b> Symptom: damaged goods set aside physically but not posted; stock the books do not show; claims never raised; the pile by the dock door that every warehouse accumulates. Correction: the rejected stock posted, claims raised on surviving evidence. Prevention: the accepted/rejected split on the receipt itself, and rejected as a warehouse the reads see.</p><p><b>Error 6 — the drifting quarantine.</b> Symptom: held stock leaking to shelf without release decisions; inspection backlogs invisible; the compliance file incomplete. Correction: the quarantine reconciled, releases documented retroactively where defensible. Prevention: release as a posted movement with a decision, and the weekly hold read.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: complete the receipt walk with honest dates, the condition split, untouched rates, and identity per lot; run the dock sequence — plan, count, inspect, capture, sign — while the driver is present; operate the rejected lane with the three resolution lanes and the claims file; state the postings — stock to two destinations, the projected transition, received-not-billed — and the correction lanes; route quality-hold classes and release by decision; and run the six receiving reads.</p><p><b>Module rule 6: the receipt states what physically arrived — counted at the dock against the order and the note, split by condition with the driver's signature on the findings, identified lot by lot from the cartons, posted at the order's rate on the arrival day, held visibly where rejected or quarantined, and claimed with evidence until every rejection resolves.</b> Module 7 covers the Purchase Invoice and the three-way match.</p>"),
]

